{
  "id": "Compound::DB00383 - Gene::1128",
  "verbalization": "Oxyphencyclimine, a selective antagonist of nicotinic acetylcholine receptors, is shown to bind to Chrm2 and Chrm3, both of which are involved in G-protein coupled acetylcholine receptor activity. This pathway leads to the activation of Chrm1, suggesting a potential role for Oxyphencyclimine in modulating the activity of Chrm1 through its interaction with these receptors. The evidence from the three paths indicates that Oxyphencyclimine may influence Chrm1 via a shared mechanism involving G-protein coupled receptors, which are critical for neurotransmitter signaling. The consistent binding to Chrm2 and Chrm3, along with their participation in the same pathway, supports a mechanism where Oxyphencyclimine exerts its effects on Chrm1 through the modulation of G-protein coupled receptor activity. The integrated evidence suggests a plausible, mechanistically sound relationship between Oxyphencyclimine and Chrm1, though further experimental validation is needed to confirm the direct interaction and functional significance.",
